CM Sommerfeld ground within 0.05 wavelengths of the surface, where NEC-2 stops
CM meeting limits that have closed-form answers.
CM A horizontal half-wave dipole 0.02 wavelengths up (41.1 mm at 145.9 MHz)
CM over ground given a conductivity of 1e10 S/m. At that conductivity the
CM half-space is a perfect conductor, so this deck must reproduce what the
CM same geometry gives with GN 1. Replace the GN card below to get it.
CM Two quantities, each against an exact answer.
CM Feedpoint resistance, against the GN 1 value the engines agree on to
CM 0.012 percent:
CM   nec2++    0.92393   (+0.77%)
CM   nec2c     1.75910   (+91.88%)
CM   aegnec2   1.75954   (+91.90%)
CM   NEC-2D    segmentation fault
CM Average power gain, which is exactly 2 for a lossless antenna over a
CM perfect conductor. All three read 1.9953 with GN 1, so 0.23 percent is the
CM angular grid's own error and the scale for the rest:
CM   nec2++    1.9802    (-0.99%)
CM   nec2c     1.0431    (-47.85%)
CM   aegnec2   1.0430    (-47.85%)
CM aegnec2 links the original Fortran SOMNEC, so this is what NEC-2 does here
CM rather than something a translation introduced. nec2++ holds to about
CM 0.01 wavelengths and then goes the same way below that; no engine tried
CM here meets either limit at 0.002 wavelengths.
CM Every sommerfeld deck already in data/ sits at 3.8 wavelengths or higher,
CM so none of them reach this regime.
CE
GW 1 11 -0.500000 0.000000 0.041091 0.500000 0.000000 0.041091 0.001000
GE -1
GN 2 0 0 0 13.000000 10000000000.000000
EX 0 1 6 0 1.000000 0.000000
FR 0 1 0 0 145.900000 0
RP 0 19 37 1001 0.000 0.000 5.000 10.000
EN
